{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the whole earth was one language, etc.</b> One must set the mind to investigate the matter of the generation of the Dispersion: what was their intent in building the city and the tower? For if their motive was some particular detail of heresy, the nature of the heresy has not been made known; nor would it have been sufficient for HaShem to punish them merely by scattering them. Moreover, one needs to understand His saying <b>nothing will be beyond their reach</b>, for one who errs might almost come to err because of it, heaven forbid. And further, what is the intention of the verse in writing for us the matter of the building of the city in its disparaged details — when surely our Rabbis of blessed memory said that it was HaShem's will that they engage in building up the world?" "It is possible that, since when the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world, He created it to be a third settled land and a third wilderness — and you will find even today many settled places that if you were to join them one to another, they would not amount to even a hundredth part of the said third of settlement, and all would be wilderness — rather, it is because the towns and cities between city and city are a proximate portion, and the people of the world travel back and forth from one to the other; by this everything is reckoned as settlement — both the towns and what lies between them — and combining all of it you find a third of settlement. Therefore it says that the whole earth was one language and unified words: they wanted everyone to be gathered in one place and not to scatter hither and thither, and that there be one place of settlement exclusively. And therefore they built the city for themselves — meaning: one city for all of them. And out of concern lest they scatter over the course of time and one of them go hither and thither, they made the tower to serve as a sign, that this is their place upon which their name is called, and also to recognize the place from afar, so that wherever one may be, coming or going, one would come toward the sign of the tower. And this is what is said: <b>lest we be scattered across the face of all the earth</b> — they did not want to be dispersed, dwindling a little here and a little there. Therefore HaShem said that this thing was not right in His eyes, for it is something they could achieve by virtue of their being possessors of free will and volition in physical action. And therefore He decreed to frustrate their counsel and confound their language, for then they would perforce be separated and each one would seek his own place. And this is what the verse says: <b>and from there HaShem scattered them across the face of all the earth.</b> And this is a faithful witness to our interpretation, that HaShem's intent was that they fill the whole world, and through this a third of the world became settled for a reason known to Him, may His name be blessed. And there is in this a sod, as is known to those who know grace (חן); and it will be almost like one who prunes the shoots and channels the flow from place to place. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded many interpretations, and the verse does not depart from its plain sense."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:26.498419503Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}